# Rules, Skills, and Agent Instruction Manifests

* From Model Capability to Repo Infrastructure

- @Key idea@: an **AI-optimized codebase** is an emergent property of designing
  for human and agent discoverability, automation, and fast feedback
  - The same properties that shorten human onboarding often shorten agent
    onboarding

- @Overview@: this lesson walks the development lifecycle
  1. Rules, skills, and instruction manifests
  2. Requirements and planning
  3. Pull request creation, splitting, and review
  4. Documentation as a machine-consumable artifact
  5. Linting, quality gates, and coverage
  6. Unit testing and golden files
  7. Build, release, and CI/CD
  8. Modularization and repository structure
  9. Dockerization and dockerized executables
  10. Production feedback and release monitoring

// TODO(gp): Look into agent-readiness benchmarks (SWE-bench, METR
//   time-horizon evals) to measure whether rules/skills/manifests actually
//   raise agent task success rate

* Why Software Engineering Is Difficult

- **No silver bullet**: complexity is both essential (the problem itself) vs.
  accidental (tools/workarounds)
  - Even if accidental complexity can shrink, essential can't *(Brooks, 1986)*

- **Invisibility**: unlike physical engineering, software has no natural
  constraints that visibly signal when a design is overbuilt or fragile

- **Combinatorial state explosion**: every added flag/variable multiplies
  possible system states beyond what humans can reason about

- **Requirements are moving targets**: building the system changes what people
  realize they actually need

- **Conway's Law**: system structure mirrors the organization's communication
  structure: human complexity leaks into technical complexity *(Conway, 1968)*

- **Software entropy**: every patch and extension nudges a system further from
  anyone's full understanding *(code rot)*

- **Brooks's Law**: adding people to a late project makes it later, due to rising
  coordination overhead *(Brooks, 1975)*

// TODO(gp): Look into tools that manage this complexity: architecture
//   decision records (ADRs), dependency-cruiser/madge for coupling, radon/
//   CodeScene for complexity metrics, Team Topologies for org design

* What Has Been Learned in 50 years of Software Engineering

- **Abstraction & information hiding**: encapsulate detail behind clean
  interfaces so no one has to hold the whole system in their head *(Parnas,
  1972)*

- **Feedback loops beat upfront planning**: short iterative cycles surface
  mistakes early, cheaper than planning them away *(Agile Manifesto, 2001)*

- **Simplicity is a discipline**: systems drift toward complexity unless actively
  resisted (e.g., YAGNI, refactoring, deliberate debt paydown)

- **Technical debt is a real tradeoff**: shortcuts accrue "interest", so teams
  must consciously choose when to pay it down *(Cunningham, 1992)*

- **Design for partial failure**: distributed systems taught the field to expect
  failure, not avoid it
  - Need retries, circuit breakers, graceful degradation

- **Observability is not optional**: you can't manage complexity you can't see
  - Logging, metrics, and tracing as first-class concerns

- **Shared mental models matter as much as code**: most fatal complexity comes
  from mismatched understanding between people, not algorithms

// TODO(gp): Look into OpenTelemetry for observability, feature flags
//   (LaunchDarkly) for partial rollout, architecture fitness functions
//   (ArchUnit) to enforce these lessons automatically

* Why Consistency and Reproducibility is Important for Sw Engineering

- @Fact@: one of the few universally agreed rules of software engineering is
  that consistency and conventions speed up development
  - A reader who knows the convention can guess the answer instead of
    searching for it

- @Definition@: **reproducibility** means the same code, config, and data
  produce the same result on any machine, at any time
  - The complement, **consistency**, means the same kind of problem is
    solved the same way everywhere in the codebase

- @Problem@: without either property, a bug that appears on one machine and
  not another burns hours before the difference is even located
  - _"Works on my machine"_ describes a symptom, not a diagnosis

- @Example@: site reliability engineering (SRE) treats consistency as a
  reliability property, not a style preference
  - A **runbook** turns a procedure that lives in one engineer's head into
    exact, ordered steps anyone on call can execute under pressure
  - A **checklist** for a deploy or a failover catches the step a tired
    engineer would otherwise skip

- @Key idea@: rules, skills, and manifests, the subject of this section, are
  how a repository encodes consistency and reproducibility for a coding
  agent, the same way a runbook encodes them for an on-call engineer

// TODO(gp): Look into reproducibility tools: Nix/Docker for pinned envs, DVC
//   for data, Backstage golden paths, Rundeck/PagerDuty for runbook
//   automation

* Why AI Agents Need Explicit Rules

// From helpers_root/CLAUDE.md
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/coding.rules.md

- @Problem@: a model completes patterns, so with no stated convention it invents
  a plausible one
  - Writes `dict` where the repo requires `Dict[str, str]`
  - Reaches for `pathlib.Path` where the repo standardizes on `os.path`
  - ...

- @Naive Solution@: restate the conventions in the prompt of every session
  - Cons: burns context on every run
  - Cons: drifts out of sync with the code, since nothing ties prompt to repo
  - Cons: cannot be reviewed, because it lives in a chat log

- @Solution@: check the conventions into the repository as versioned files
  - `.claude/rules.md` maps _file type_ $\to$ _rule file_
  - `<TOPIC>.rules.md` holds why and when for principles and decision criteria
  - `.claude/templates/<TOPIC>.template.<EXT>` holds what to copy: a skeleton
    with `<VAR>` placeholders

- @Key idea@: rules stored in the repo are reviewed, diffed, and versioned like
  code, so agent behavior changes through a pull request

// TODO(gp): Look into the emerging AGENTS.md open standard, Cursor's
//   `.cursorrules`, GitHub Copilot custom instructions, and how ruff/mypy
//   already enforce some conventions mechanically instead of by prose

* CLAUDE.md as a Machine-Readable Instruction Manifest

// From helpers_root/CLAUDE.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

- @Definition@: an **agent instruction manifest** is a structured description of
  architecture, module boundaries, and canonical commands, written for a program
  rather than for a newcomer

- @Components@: what a manifest declares
  - _Architecture_: components and ownership boundaries
  - _Conventions_: module naming, import style, test layout
  - _Canonical commands_: the blessed way to test, lint, and enter a container
  - _Pointers_: the rule files that govern each file type

- @Pros@
  - The agent is productive without an interactive onboarding loop
  - Maintainers evolve agent behavior through code review, not a hidden prompt
  - Context cost drops, since the manual is retrieved instead of pasted

- @Remark@: this is not documentation in the traditional sense
  - It is an operating manual whose primary reader is a machine

// TODO(gp): Look into the emerging AGENTS.md open standard, Cursor's
//   `.cursorrules`, GitHub Copilot custom instructions, and how ruff/mypy
//   already enforce some conventions mechanically instead of by prose

* Scaling Context Retrieval Beyond a Static Manifest

- @Problem@: `CLAUDE.md` and `<TOPIC>.rules.md` work while the manifest fits
  in context; a codebase with hundreds of rule files and thousands of source
  files stops fitting no matter how well it is organized

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack retrieves the _right_ rule file or the
  _right_ prior implementation on demand, so the agent either loads
  everything or relies on the file paths it happens to already know

- @What it would take@
  - Semantic or embedding-based search over rules, skills, and code, so a
    query like "how do we name test classes" resolves to
    `testing.rules.md` without enumerating `.claude/skills/`
  - Retrieval that respects the same layering `helpers` already enforces
    for imports, so a low-level rule is not buried under high-level noise

- @Remark@: the repository's own backlog names this gap directly (`ctx7?`
  in `resources.md`), unresolved as of this lesson

- @Key idea@: a manifest is a whiteboard, not a library; past a certain repo
  size, an agent needs an index, not a longer document

// TODO(gp): Look into embedding-based code/doc search that would close this
//   gap: Sourcegraph Cody context, a Context7-style MCP server, LlamaIndex
//   or txtai for retrieval-augmented rule lookup

* Skill Files: Packaging Repeatable Workflows as Rules

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/skill.rules.md

- @Problem@: a rule file says what "good" looks like, but not how to carry out a
  recurring task

- @Definition@: a **skill** is a named, versioned procedure stored at
  `.claude/skills/<TOPIC>.<ACTION>/SKILL.md`
  - E.g., `testing.write_unit_tests`, `docker.shrink_container`

- @Components@: the sections of a `SKILL.md`
  - _Frontmatter_: `description` and `model`
  - _Goal_: one or two sentences
  - _Workflow_: numbered, imperative steps
  - _Conventions_: a pointer to `<TOPIC>.rules.md`, never a restatement
  - _Verification_: how to check the work was done

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Artifact", "Answers", "Example"),
  rows: (
    ("Rule file", "Why and when", "coding.rules.md"),
    ("Skill file", "How, step by step", "coding.rename/SKILL.md"),
    ("Template", "What to copy", "coding.template.py"),
  ),
  caption: "Three artifact types, three questions",
)
```

- @Key idea@: naming a workflow turns tacit practice into an asset the team can
  review, test, and improve

// TODO(gp): Look into LangChain tools/agents, MCP servers, and prompt/skill
//   registries (LangGraph, Semantic Kernel plugins) as comparable ways to
//   package a repeatable workflow for an agent

* repo_config.yaml: Machine-Readable Repository Contracts

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md
// From
// helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.transition_to_repo_config_yml.how_to_guide.md

- @Problem@: an agent asked to build and push an image must answer _"which image
  name?"_ and _"which registry?"_
  - Prose documentation forces search and inference, and both can be wrong

- @Definition@: `repo_config.yaml` is a **machine-readable contract** that
  encodes the operational identity of a runnable directory
  ```yaml
  repo_info:
    repo_name: csfy
  docker_info:
    docker_image_name: csfy
  container_registry_info:
    ghcr: ghcr.io/causify-ai
  runnable_dir_info:
    use_helpers_as_nested_module: 1
    venv_tag: helpers
  ```

- @Remark@: the file replaced an executable `repo_config.py`
  - Config is now _data_, read through `hrecouti.get_repo_config()` or `hserver`
  - `IS_SUPER_REPO` was renamed `USE_HELPERS_AS_NESTED_MODULE` for clarity

- @Key idea@: a release task derives the registry and the naming convention from
  the contract, instead of relying on environment-specific assumptions

// TODO(gp): Look into other machine-readable repo contracts: Backstage
//   `catalog-info.yaml`, Nx `project.json`, Bazel `BUILD` files, cookiecutter
//   config

# Requirements and Planning

* From Issue to Specification: Automating Requirements Capture

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/specs.write

- @Problem@: an agent handed a one-line issue title fills the gap between
  "what was asked" and "what to build" with assumptions, and a wrong
  assumption compounds every commit downstream

- @Definition@: a **specification** is the artifact that separates "what
  does the user want" from "how is it implemented", written before a branch
  exists

- @Solution@: the `specs.write` skill drafts a specification from a request,
  covering scope, interface, edge cases, and out-of-scope items explicitly

- @Constraints@: what keeps the spec from becoming its own source of drift
  - Stop for human approval before implementation starts
  - Reference the spec file from the pull request, so reviewers check code
    against a written contract, not against the issue title

- @Key idea@: the earliest and cheapest place to catch a misunderstanding is
  before the first line of code, not in review

// TODO(gp): Look into spec-writing conventions this borrows from: RFC
//   templates (Google/AWS), Shape Up pitch docs, Amazon's PR/FAQ, Jira/Linear
//   spec templates

* Architecture Decision Records: Capturing Why, Not Just What

- @Problem@: a repository's rules and manifests capture the current
  convention, but not why an earlier alternative was rejected, so an agent
  re-litigates a settled tradeoff (e.g., sibling vs. child containers) every
  time it is asked to reconsider one

- @Definition@: an **architecture decision record (ADR)** is a short,
  dated, versioned file recording one decision: the context, the options
  considered, the choice, and the consequences

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack stores decisions as structured, queryable
  artifacts; the reasoning lives in pull request discussions and chat
  threads an agent cannot cheaply retrieve

- @What it would take@
  - A `docs/adr/NNNN-<slug>.md` convention with a fixed template
  - A skill that drafts an ADR from a pull request discussion and links it
    from the affected rule file

- @Key idea@: a rule file says what to do today, an ADR says why today's
  answer beat yesterday's, and an agent needs both to avoid reopening
  closed questions

// TODO(gp): Look into ADR tooling that could fill this gap: adr-tools CLI,
//   Log4brains, the MADR template, and linking ADRs from Backstage TechDocs

# Code Modularization and Repo Structure

* Single vs Multiple Repo: The Monorepo Tradeoff

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.integrate_repos.how_to_guide.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 2

- @Problem@: teams want the _modularity_ of many repositories and the
  _environment consistency_ of one, and the classic answer sacrifices one

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Approach", "Pros", "Cons"),
  rows: (
    ("Monorepo",
     "One commit history, no version skew, shared tooling",
     "Long CI builds, extra build tools, weak boundaries, hard permissions"),
    ("Multi-repo",
     "Independent lifecycles, small checkouts, clear ownership",
     "Dependency skew across repos, costly change propagation"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.7fr, 1.3fr, 1.5fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: when two forks of a repository must be reconciled, the work is
  scripted rather than manual
  - `i integrate_create_branch --dir-basename cmamp1` starts the integration
    branch on each side
  - Integrate by directory when a directory was touched on one side only

- @Key idea@: the real question is not "one repo or many", it is "what is the
  smallest unit that can be built, tested, and released on its own"

// TODO(gp): Look into monorepo tooling (Bazel, Nx, Turborepo, Lerna) and
//   multi-repo tooling (git submodules/subtrees, Meta's Sapling) for how
//   other stacks solve the same tradeoff

* Runnable Directories: A Hybrid Architecture

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Parts 2 and 4

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Definition@: a directory is **runnable** when it carries its own `devops`
  directory, and therefore its own container and dependencies

- @Components@: what a runnable directory contains
  - `devops`: build, run, and release the container
  - `dev_scripts_<name>`: thin environment and `setenv`
  - `repo_config.yaml`: the machine-readable contract
  - Common files symlinked back to `//helpers`

- @Fact@: imports still start at the parent repository root, so a runnable
  directory stays usable both alone and as part of the whole

::::
:::: {.column width=46%}

```graphviz
digraph RunnableRepo {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.26;
  ranksep=0.40;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
