  Dev  [label="dev container",
        fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Tool [label="tool container\n(sibling)",
        fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Kid  [label="tool container\n(child, privileged)",
        fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];

  Host -> Dev;
  Host -> Tool [label="  via mounted socket"];
  Dev -> Tool [style=dashed, label="  requests"];
  Dev -> Kid [label="  nested daemon"];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key idea@: the sibling pattern trades isolation for security and cache reuse,
  which is the right trade for tooling rather than for untrusted code

// TODO(gp): Look into Podman (daemonless/rootless) as a sibling alternative,
//   sysbox for safer Docker-in-Docker, and Kaniko/Buildah for daemonless
//   image builds in CI

* Optimizing and Shrinking Containers with AI

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/docker.shrink_requirements
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/docker.shrink_container

- @Problem@: images accumulate packages nobody removes, and every CI run pays
  for the extra build and pull time

- @Algorithm@: how `docker.shrink_requirements` proceeds
  1. Extract the real imports from every `.py` file by grep or AST analysis
  2. Map the distribution name to the import name, e.g., `Pillow` $\to$ `PIL`,
     `scikit-learn` $\to$ `sklearn`, `PyYAML` $\to$ `yaml`
  3. Also inspect `setup.py`, `pyproject.toml`, `tasks.py`, and `Makefile` for
     tool-level use
  4. Emit a table of
     (`package`, `import_name`, `used_in_code`, `verdict`)
  5. Write the trimmed `requirements.txt`

- @Constraints@: what must be flagged and not removed
  - Plugins loaded dynamically through `importlib`
  - Transitive dependencies pulled in by another listed package
  - Packages used only by tests, marked `test-only`

- @Key idea@: the `verdict` column is the design choice, since it turns a
  destructive edit into a reviewable proposal

// TODO(gp): Look into dive and docker-slim for image-layer inspection, and
//   Trivy/Snyk Container for size and vulnerability scanning together

# Automating the Pull Request Lifecycle

* From Manual PRs to Agent-Driven PR Creation

// From helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md

- @Definition@: the unit of work is a **GitHub issue**, implemented in a branch,
  and delivered as a pull request whose title matches the branch name

- @Components@: the labels that encode review state
  - `PR_for_authors`: changes are owed by the author
  - `PR_for_reviewers`: ready for team-leader review
  - `PR_for_integrators`: final round, close to merge

- @Example@: the same three commands a human runs
  ```bash
  > i git_branch_create -i 828
  > i gh_create_pr
  > i gh_watch
  ```

- @Remark@: reference the issue as _"Related to #NNN"_, never _"Fixes #NNN"_
  - GitHub auto-closes a linked issue on merge, and closing is a human decision

- @Key idea@: the workflow was already scripted for humans, so an agent executes
  the identical path with no special-case automation

// TODO(gp): Look into `gh` CLI scripting, GitHub Actions bots, and agent PR
//   platforms (OpenAI Codex Cloud, Devin, Cursor background agents) that
//   automate this same path

* Splitting Work: Child PRs and Branch Splitting

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/github.split_branch_in_PRs
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/github.create_child_pr

- @Problem@: a long-lived branch mixes refactoring with behavior change, and the
  reviewer cannot separate _what moved_ from _what changed_

- @Goal@: decompose `origin/master...HEAD` into small, cohesive pull requests
  that minimize review complexity and the risk of breaking tests

- @Algorithm@
  1. List the touched files with `git diff --name-status origin/master HEAD`
  2. Group them so each pull request is internally coupled
  3. Prefer whole files per pull request over splitting one file across several
  4. Keep a source file and its test file in the same pull request
  5. Order the pull requests so low-risk, wide-touch changes merge first
  6. Emit `pr<NUM>.files.txt` and an executable `pr<NUM>.pytest.sh` per unit
  7. Record the plan in `github_PR_plan.md` and re-lint it

- @Example@: carrying a subset of files into a fresh child branch
  ```bash
  > i git_branch_next_name
  > git_sync_between_branches.sh --src_branch $SRC --dst_branch $DST \
      --files "$FILES"
  ```

- @Key idea@: the mechanics are automatable, but the judgment of what belongs
  together stays under review

// TODO(gp): Look into stacked-diff tools that automate this split: Graphite,
//   git-branchless, Sapling, ghstack

* Coding While You Sleep: Remote and Autonomous Agents

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/resources.md: Managing Code

- @Definition@: in **asynchronous agent work** no human is attached to the
  session while the agent runs

- @Techniques@
  - Web and desktop sessions that outlive the terminal
  - Remote control of a running session from a phone
  - Scheduled tasks that fire on a cron expression
  - A GitHub app that dispatches an agent from an issue or a comment

- @Assumptions@: conditions that make the mode acceptable
  - The permission surface is bounded, so destructive actions need approval
  - Every gate that CI runs can also be run by the agent before it reports
  - A human still merges

- @Challenges@
  - The agent cannot ask a clarifying question, so ambiguity is resolved by
    guessing
  - The blast radius grows with session length
  - Review load moves in time, not away: the work still arrives on a human desk

- @Key idea@: asynchronous work changes the human role from _author_ to
  _reviewer of evidence_

// TODO(gp): Look into Devin, OpenAI Codex Cloud, Claude Code's GitHub Action
//   and remote control, Cursor background agents, and Sweep.dev as examples
//   of this mode

* Governing Agent Permissions and Auditing Actions

- @Problem@: the asynchronous-agent slide already names the risk, "the
  blast radius grows with session length", but names no mechanism that
  bounds or records it

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack scopes what a long-running or scheduled
  agent is allowed to touch, or logs what it actually did, independent of
  the pull request it eventually opens

- @What it would take@
  - A permission manifest per agent run: which directories, which
    commands, which external calls are in scope
  - An audit log of tool calls and decisions, retained even when the
    session produces no pull request
  - A budget on cost or token spend per run, so a stuck agent fails loud
    instead of running unattended

- @Key idea@: a human reviews the diff an agent produces; nothing today
  lets a human review the _actions_ an agent took to get there

// TODO(gp): Look into OPA/Rego for a permission manifest, sandboxes
//   (Firecracker, gVisor) to bound blast radius, and OpenTelemetry traces of
//   tool calls as the audit log

* Automating PR Review with AI (1/2)

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.ai_review.how_to_guide.md

- @Goal@: turn a review pass into a list of located, actionable comments instead
  of prose

- @Definition@: a **cfile** is a vim quickfix list of `file:line:message`
  entries, generated by a tool and navigated by a human

- @Algorithm@
  1. Commit first, so the transform lands in its own reviewable commit
  2. `invoke git_branch_diff -t base --only-print-files`
  3. `ai_review.py -i $FILE -p $PROMPT` to emit the `cfile`
  4. `vim -c "cfile cfile"` to walk the comments and discard false positives
  5. `inject_todos.py --cfile cfile` to leave `TODO`s for whoever fixes them
  6. `apply_todos.py` to let a model apply them, which is the risky step

- @Variants@: the prompt selects the lens
  - `review_correctness`, `review_architecture`, `review_linter`, `review_llm`

- @Key idea@: the review criteria live in the same style-guide file the linter
  reads, so tool and reviewer share one source of truth

// TODO(gp): Look into reviewdog for turning linter output into inline PR
//   comments, and Danger/danger.js for policy-as-code review rules

* Automating PR Review with AI (2/2)

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.code_review_tools.reference.md
// From helpers_root/docs/code_guidelines/all.code_review.how_to_guide.md

- @Examples@: hosted review systems
  - **GitHub Copilot Code Review**: in-platform, minimal setup, shallow analysis
  - **Graphite**: pull request workflow and stacked diffs, light on semantics
  - **CodeRabbit**: deeper logic and bug analysis, younger integrations
  - **Snyk Code**: static analysis aimed at security and anti-patterns

- @Limitations@
  - Suggestions are best guesses and need human oversight
  - Project-specific context is exactly what a hosted tool lacks
  - Pricing is per seat per month, so cost scales linearly with team size

- @Fact@: the human checklist is not automated away
  - Is the design right, and is the code more complex than it needs to be?
  - Are the tests well designed, and do comments explain _why_ not _what_?
  - Is the developer building something they do not need yet?

- @Key idea@: automate the mechanical pass, and spend the human pass on
  architecture, specification conformity, and intent

// TODO(gp): Look into Codacy, DeepSource, Qodo (CodiumAI), Greptile, and the
//   open-source PR-Agent as further hosted or self-hosted reviewers

* The Agentic Loop: Plan, Patch, Prove, Summarize

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}

- @Definition@: the **agentic loop** is the disciplined workflow the
  infrastructure makes possible
  - _Plan_: state the intended change and the affected runnable directory
  - _Patch_: implement a minimal diff that respects directory boundaries
  - _Prove_: run the cheapest gate first, then widen
  - _Summarize_: produce a pull-request-ready explanation with evidence

- @Example@: the escalation order inside _Prove_
  ```bash
  > invoke lint --modified
  > invoke run_fast_tests
  > invoke run_slow_tests
  ```

::::
:::: {.column width=44%}

```graphviz
digraph AgenticLoop {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.28;
  ranksep=0.42;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.20,0.12",
        height=0.46];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  // plan : teal | patch : blue | prove : sage green | summary : orange

  Plan  [label="Plan",
         fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
  Patch [label="Patch",
         fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  Prove [label="Prove\n(lint, tests)",
         fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Sum   [label="Summarize",
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

  Plan -> Patch;
  Patch -> Prove;
  Prove -> Sum [label="  gates green"];
  Prove -> Patch [label="  gate fails",
                  style=dashed, penwidth=1.6,
                  color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B",
                  constraint=false];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key idea@: the novelty is not that an agent writes code, it is that the
  change arrives carrying its own evidence

// TODO(gp): Look into SWE-agent, Aider's edit-test loop, and ReAct-style
//   plan/act/observe agents as other implementations of this loop

* Automating Root-Cause Analysis When a Gate Fails

- @Problem@: the agentic loop's dashed edge, "gate fails $\to$ back to
  Patch", hides the hardest step; knowing a test failed is not the same as
  knowing why, and an agent that patches without a cause thrashes

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack automates the diagnosis itself
  - No bisection: which commit in the branch introduced the failure
  - No log or stack-trace clustering: is this failure the same one from
    three pull requests ago, or new

- @What it would take@
  - `git bisect` driven by the agent, running the failing test at each step
  - A skill that groups CI failures by stack-trace similarity, so a
    recurring flake is recognized instead of re-diagnosed from zero

- @Key idea@: "Prove" tells the agent a gate is red; closing the loop needs
  a step that tells it _why_, or Patch is just another guess

// TODO(gp): Look into automated `git bisect` runners, Sentry/Datadog error
//   clustering by stack-trace fingerprint, and BuildPulse for grouping
//   recurring CI failures

# Automating Documentation

* Documentation as a First-Class, Machine-Consumable Artifact

// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.diataxis.explanation.md
// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md

- @Problem@: an essay-shaped document is expensive for a human to skim and
  expensive for a retriever to chunk

- @Definition@: **Diataxis** splits documentation into four modes, each with one
  job

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Mode", "Orientation", "Reader question", "Suffix"),
  rows: (
    ("Tutorial", "Learning", "Teach me by doing", ".tutorial.md"),
    ("How-to guide", "Goal", "Help me do this task", ".how_to_guide.md"),
    ("Reference", "Information", "Tell me the details", ".reference.md"),
    ("Explanation", "Understanding", "Explain the design", ".explanation.md"),
  ),
)
```

- @Techniques@: conventions that make a document machine-consumable
  - One level-1 header, a summary in bullets, a linter-generated table of
    contents
  - File name encodes the mode:
    `docs/{component}/{audience}.{topic}.{diataxis_tag}.md`
  - One idea per bullet, diagrams instead of wordy explanations

- @Key idea@: the structure that makes a document skimmable for a human is the
  same structure that makes it easily searchable and consumable for an AI
  assistant

// TODO(ai_gp): Prefer markdown

// TODO(gp): Look into doc tooling: Docusaurus/MkDocs renderers, Vale/alex for
//   prose linting, and RAG doc-indexing tools (LlamaIndex, Context7) for
//   agent retrieval

* Generating and Maintaining READMEs with AI

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/readme.create
// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.markdown_tools.explanation.md

- @Problem@: a `README` is written once and rots, because nothing fails when it
  goes stale

- Conventions for README (dir vs file)
  - Summary, structure of the directory, description of files
  - Description of executables, workflows, and architecture when they apply
- Checks links
- Checks other markdown links
- Check that it's referred by other files
- Keep it updated (incrementally when something change + a top to bottom run)

- @Key idea@: generation is cheap, so the scarce resource is a check that the
  document still matches the code

// TODO(ai_gp): Systems to keep documentation updated
// ctx7?

// TODO(gp): Look into readme-ai, Mintlify writer, and link checkers
//   (markdown-link-check, lychee) for automated README generation and upkeep

# Automating Code Quality

* Pre-commit Tool

// TODO(gp): Look into the pre-commit framework (pre-commit.com) and how it
//   compares to this repo's custom `pre-commit.py` hook runner

* Pre-Commit Hooks

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git_hooks.reference.md
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git_hooks.how_to_guide.md

::: columns
:::: {.column width=54%}

- @Definition@: a **git hook** is a script Git runs around an event such as a
  commit
  - `pre-commit.py` runs before the commit is created
  - `commit-msg.py` checks and edits the commit message

- @Components@: what the pre-commit hook enforces
  - `check_master`: never commit on the default branch
  - `check_author`: name and email are configured
  - `check_file_size`: refuse oversized blobs
  - `check_python_compile`: the file at least parses
  - `check_gitleaks`: no secret in the staged diff
  - Leftover merge-conflict markers, for any Git operation that can create them

- @Remark@: the escape hatch is explicit
  - `git commit --no-verify` skips the checks for one commit
  - `enable_git_commit_hook: False` in `repo_config.yaml` disables them per repo

::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph QualityGates {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.24;
  ranksep=0.34;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=11,
        margin="0.18,0.10",
        height=0.42];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.7,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=9,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  // local gate : sage green | remote gate : blue | outcome : orange

  Hook  [label="Pre-commit hook\nseconds",
         fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Lint  [label="Linter\nseconds",
         fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Fast  [label="Fast tests\n5 s per class",
         fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Slow  [label="Slow tests\n30 s per class",
         fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  CI    [label="CI: lint, tests,\ngitleaks, semgrep",
         fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  Merge [label="Merge",
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

  Hook -> Lint -> Fast -> Slow -> CI -> Merge;
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key idea@: gates are ordered by cost, so the cheapest check rejects the most
  common mistake first

// TODO(gp): Look into husky and lefthook as alternative hook managers for
//   JS/multi-language repos, and Talisman for a hook dedicated to
//   secret-scanning

* Type Checking as a Quality Gate

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/coding.fix_pyright

- @Problem@: a linter checks style and a test checks behavior for the paths
  it exercises; neither one catches a value of the wrong type flowing
  through a path no test happens to cover

- @Solution@: `coding.fix_pyright` runs static type checking and fixes the
  reported lints, closing the gap between "style is correct" and "types
  are correct"

- @Constraints@: what keeps a type-checking pass honest
  - A fix narrows a type or adds an annotation, it does not silence the
    check with a blanket `# type: ignore`
  - Third-party stubs are missing as often as they are wrong, so a
    suppression at a boundary is flagged, not hidden

- @Key idea@: type checking is static analysis for correctness, not style,
  and belongs in the same escalation ladder as the linter

// TODO(gp): Look into the mypy daemon (`dmypy`) and pyright watch mode for
//   fast incremental checks, and typing-coverage reporters to track
//   annotation completeness over time

* Security Gates: Secrets and Static Analysis

// From helpers_root/docs/build_system/all.gitleaks_workflow.explanation.md
// From helpers_root/docs/build_system/all.semgrep_workflow.explanation.md

- @Problem@: an autonomous agent that edits configuration files can commit a
  credential, and deleting the line later does not remove it from history

- @Solution 1: gitleaks@: secret scanning at two points
  - Locally as the `check_gitleaks` pre-commit check
  - In continuous integration on every pull request, every push to `master`, on
    a daily schedule, and on manual dispatch
  - Rules in `.github/gitleaks-rules.toml`, failures posted to a Slack channel

- @Solution 2: semgrep@: pattern-based static analysis
  - Uses the `p/secrets` rule pack, with the same trigger schedule
  - `.semgrepignore` excludes vendored code, `# nosemgrep` suppresses one line
  - Reproduce a failure locally with `semgrep --config=p/secrets`

- @Key idea@: autonomy is acceptable exactly where the failure mode has an
  automated detector

// TODO(gp): Look into TruffleHog and detect-secrets as gitleaks alternatives,
//   and GitHub secret scanning plus CodeQL/Bandit for broader SAST coverage

* Dependency Vulnerability Scanning, SBOMs, and Automated Upgrades

- @Problem@: `docker.shrink_requirements` removes packages nobody imports,
  but says nothing about a package everybody imports that carries a known
  CVE, or one whose license conflicts with the project's

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack scans dependencies for known
  vulnerabilities, produces a software bill of materials (SBOM), or opens
  an upgrade pull request on its own

- @What it would take@
  - A scanner run against a CVE database on the same schedule as
    `gitleaks` and `semgrep`, failing the build on a high-severity match
  - A generated SBOM per release, so "are we exposed to CVE-X" is a
    lookup, not an audit
  - A bot that opens a pull request bumping one dependency at a time, with
    the changelog attached, for a human to merge

- @Key idea@: `docker.shrink_requirements` answers "do we need this
  package"; a vulnerability scanner answers the equally necessary "is this
  package safe", and today only the first question is automated

// TODO(gp): Look into Dependabot and Renovate for automated upgrade PRs,
//   Snyk/Trivy/Grype for CVE scanning, and Syft or OSV-Scanner for SBOM
//   generation

* Code Coverage Tracking and Enforcement

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.codecov.how_to_guide.md
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.code_coverage_subprocess.how_to_guide.md

- @Definition@: coverage is collected per **test suite**, and each suite uploads
  under its own flag
  - `fast` and `slow` run daily, `superslow` runs weekly

- @Example@: measuring locally before pushing
  ```bash
  > invoke run_coverage --suite fast --generate-html-report
  > invoke run_coverage_subprocess --target-dir=dev_scripts_helpers/llms
  ```

- @Techniques@: covering subprocesses and containers
  - `.coveragerc` sets `parallel = True` and
    `concurrency = multiprocessing`
  - `hcoverage.inject()` installs the startup hook, `COVERAGE_PROCESS_START`
    points at the config
  - `hcoverage.coverage_combine()` merges the per-process data files

- @Fact@: the gate is a delta, not an absolute
  - The project check fails when coverage drops by 1% or more against the base
  - `carryforward` reuses the last report for a suite that did not run
  - The patch check always scores the lines changed by the pull request

- @Limitations@: coverage measures which lines ran, not whether anything was
  asserted about them

// TODO(gp): Look into Coveralls as a Codecov alternative, and mutation
//   testing (next slide) as the tool that actually answers this limitation

* Mutation Testing: Verifying That Tests Actually Assert

- @Problem@: the coverage slide's own limitation stands unanswered:
  coverage measures which lines ran, not whether anything was asserted
  about them, and a test that calls a function and checks nothing scores
  full coverage

- @Definition@: **mutation testing** injects a small fault into the source
  (flip a comparison, drop a line) and reruns the suite; a test suite that
  still passes did not actually exercise that logic

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack runs a mutation pass; coverage is the only
  signal a test's thoroughness produces today

- @What it would take@
  - A mutation runner scoped to the lines a pull request changed, since a
    full-repository mutation run is too slow to gate a merge
  - A `mutation_score` reported alongside coverage, so "100% covered, 40%
    mutation-killed" is visible instead of hidden behind one green number

- @Key idea@: coverage proves a line executed, mutation testing proves a
  test would have noticed if that line were wrong

// TODO(gp): Look into mutmut and cosmic-ray for Python, Stryker Mutator for
//   JS, and Pitest for Java as the mutation runners that would fill this gap

# Automating Unit Testing

* Testing Philosophy: Tests as Executable Specifications

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/testing.rules.md

- @Key idea@: a test is a specification a machine can check, which makes it the
  only feedback channel an agent can act on without a human

- @Characteristics@: the four invariants
  - _Test one thing_: one class per function or class, one case per method
  - _Self-contained_: never assume execution order, state inputs explicitly
  - _Outside-in_: interface tests survive refactors, implementation tests do not
  - _In sync_: renaming the tested class renames the test class and the file

- @Example@: every method uses the same four sections
  ```python
  def test1(self) -> None:
      """
      Test extraction from a valid file path.
      """
      # Prepare inputs.
      file_path = "msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10-Name.md"
      # Prepare outputs.
      expected_dir = "msml610"
      # Run test.
      actual_dir, _ = _extract_lesson_from_file(file_path)
      # Check outputs.
      self.assertEqual(actual_dir, expected_dir)
  ```

- @Remark@: naming is fully determined, so there is nothing to decide
  - `parse_limit_range()` $\to$ `Test_parse_limit_range`, `Config` $\to$
    `TestConfig`, methods numbered `test1`, `test2` with the intent in the
    docstring

// TODO(gp): Look into Hypothesis for property-based testing and Pact for
//   contract testing as complements to these example-based specs

* Writing and Extending Unit Tests with AI

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/testing.write_unit_tests
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/testing.reach_coverage

- @Problem@: asked for tests with no constraint, a model produces many shallow
  tests that execute code without asserting behavior

- @Algorithm@: both skills are plan-then-implement, with a human gate in between
  1. Establish the baseline, e.g., run coverage and list uncovered lines
  2. Map each uncovered path to a proposed test
  3. Specify class names, method names, inputs, and expected behavior, with no
     code
  4. Stop and wait for approval
  5. Implement, re-measure, and report the remaining gaps

- @Techniques@: what the plan is told to avoid
  - Testing assertions, defensive checks, and invalid inputs unless correctness
    depends on them
  - Mocking anything internal: mock third-party providers, cloud infrastructure,
    databases, and external APIs, and patch at the call site

- @Key idea@: the approval gate is where a human injects intent, before the
  expensive step, not after

// TODO(gp): Look into CodiumAI/Qodo Cover, Diffblue Cover, EvoSuite, and
//   Copilot test generation as AI-driven test writers

* Golden File Testing and Test Reporting

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4
// From helpers_root/docs/build_system/all.pytest_allure.explanation.md

- @Definition@: a **golden file** stores the full expected output of a test in
  `test/outcomes/<TestClass.test_method>/output/test.txt`

- @Pros@: why whole-output comparison suits agents
  - The diff is concrete, so the model can judge whether the change was intended
  - `pytest --update_outcomes` makes accepting an intentional change one step
  - Side effects far from the edit show up immediately as unexpected diffs
  - Capturing the entire output makes an incorrectly passing test unlikely

- @Remark@: the current convention prefers `self.assert_equal()` against an
  expected string written in the test over `self.check_string()`
  - The expectation stays visible in the file the reviewer is reading
  - Both styles share the rule: compare the whole output, never piece by piece

- @Techniques@: reporting on top of the raw results
  - `pytest --alluredir=allure-results` emits result and container files
  - The Allure command-line tool renders HTML, and stored history enables trends
  - Trends answer _"did this test just start failing?"_, which a single run
    cannot

// TODO(gp): Look into syrupy for pytest snapshot testing, ApprovalTests for
//   the approval-testing style, and ReportPortal/TestRail as Allure
//   alternatives

* Detecting and Quarantining Flaky Tests

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/pytest.triage_local_unit_tests
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/pytest.triage_github_unit_tests

- @Problem@: the buildmeister policy is "fix it or revert" within one hour,
  but a test that fails intermittently is neither clearly broken nor
  clearly fine, and re-running it by hand burns the same hour it is meant
  to protect

- @Solution@: `pytest.triage_local_unit_tests` and
  `pytest.triage_github_unit_tests` analyze a failure locally or in CI and
  classify it before a human is paged

- @Algorithm@
  1. Re-run the failing test in isolation and repeated, to separate
     order-dependence from true flakiness
  2. Compare the failure against the Allure history for the same test class
  3. Classify: genuine regression, environment flake, or known-flaky
  4. Genuine regressions file a build-fail issue; known flakes are
     quarantined with a tracking issue, not silently skipped

- @Key idea@: a flaky test left in the suite trains the team to ignore red,
  which is a worse outcome than a quarantined test with an open issue

// TODO(gp): Look into BuildPulse and Datadog Test Optimization for flaky-test
//   detection dashboards, and pytest-rerunfailures for automatic retry-based
//   triage

# Automating Build, Release, and CI/CD

* Invoke Workflows: A Centralized Task Registry

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.invoke_workflows.how_to_guide.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

- @Definition@: `invoke` provides Makefile-like targets written in Python
  - Tasks are `@task`-decorated functions in `helpers/lib_tasks_*.py`, exported
    through `tasks.py`

- @Components@: namespaces name the topic
  - `docker_*`, `git_*`, `gh_*`, `run_*`, `lint_*`, `integrate_*`

- @Example@: discovery is a command, not a document
  ```bash
  > invoke --list
  > invoke --help run_fast_tests
  > i run_fast_tests --coverage
  ```

- @Pros@: what a task registry gives an agent
  - _Discoverability_: enumerate every operation without prior knowledge
  - _No ambiguity_: call a named task instead of synthesizing a Docker command
  - _Consistency_: the same commands work in every runnable directory
  - _Encoded practice_: container choice, pytest options, and timeouts are
    already correct

- @Remark@: a task runs only where it is imported into `tasks.py`, which makes
  the runnable directory the unit of execution

// TODO(gp): Look into Make, Just, Taskfile.dev, and Nx `run-many` as
//   alternative centralized task registries

* CI/CD Automation and the Buildmeister Role

// From helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.buildmeister.how_to_guide.md
// From helpers_root/docs/build_system/all.linter_gh_workflow.explanation.md

- @Problem@: automation multiplies the changes landing per day, and a red build
  blocks every one of them

- @Definition@: the **buildmeister** is a rotating role that owns build health

- @Components@: the duties of the rotation
  - Check the dashboard daily and triage every break notification first
  - File an issue titled `Build fail - {repo} {test type} ({run number})` with
    the `FAILED` list, the stack trace, and the link to the run
  - Push the responsible person, and run a post-mortem on the cause

- @Fact@: the policy is _"fix it or revert"_
  - The build returns to green within one hour
  - Disabling a test is the last resort and never a unilateral decision

- @Example@: the linter gate encodes the same idea in code
  ```bash
  > invoke git_fetch_master
  > invoke lint --branch
  > invoke lint_check_if_it_was_run
  ```

- @Key idea@: an automated fleet still needs a human owner for the shared signal

// TODO(gp): Look into build-health dashboards (Datadog CI Visibility, GitHub
//   required-status-checks) and flaky-test quarantine tools (BuildPulse) that
//   support this role

* Automating Container Releases

// From
// helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.devops_docker_auto_release.explanation.md

- @Goal@: release a development image on a schedule with no human running the
  steps

- @Algorithm@: three phases with one human gate
  1. _Build and test_: a weekly workflow calls
     `invoke docker_build_test_dev_image`, which bumps the version, opens an
     issue and a branch, builds the image, runs tests, updates `changelog.txt`,
     opens a pull request with reviewers, and pushes `dev-{version}` to GHCR
  2. _Manual review_: the team validates changelog, version, and lockfile,
     optionally pulls the candidate image, then merges
  3. _Automated release_: the merge event plus the `Automated release` label
     triggers re-tagging and pushing to every target registry

- @Remark@: review is requested from a team, not a person
  - `release_team` in `repo_config.yaml` removes the single point of failure

- @Key idea@: the human gate sits between _built and tested_ and _published_,
  which is the only irreversible step

// TODO(gp): Look into semantic-release, Release Please, and changesets for
//   automated versioning and changelog generation applied to container
//   releases

* Docker Container Release Flow: Dev to Prod

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.devops_docker.how_to_guide.md
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.devops_docker.reference.md

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Definition@: a **stage** is a step in the release workflow of an image
  - _local_: a private release candidate for one developer or server
  - _dev_: blessed image used by the team and by CI, with the source
    bind-mounted so code changes need no rebuild
  - _prod_: self-contained image with the code copied inside, built from `dev`

- @Components@: the invoke targets
  - `docker_build_local_image`
  - `docker_tag_local_image_as_dev`
  - `docker_push_dev_image`
  - `docker_build_prod_image`
  - `docker_release_all`
  - `docker_rollback_dev_image`

::::
:::: {.column width=46%}

```graphviz
digraph ReleaseFlow {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.26;
  ranksep=0.40;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
