# Production Feedback and Release Monitoring

* Closing the Loop: Feeding Production Incidents Back to the Agent

- @Problem@: the lifecycle in this lesson ends at "Merge" and "push to
  registry"; nothing routes a production error back to a filed issue, so
  the feedback loop that catches a mistake before merge does not exist
  after merge

- @Gap@: an exception in production and a failing assertion in CI are the
  same signal, "the code did something wrong", but only the second one
  reaches an agent automatically today

- @What it would take@
  - An alerting rule that opens a GitHub issue from a production error,
    with the stack trace, the request that triggered it, and the deployed
    version attached
  - The issue routes into the same `PR_for_authors` labeling scheme
    already used for human-filed work, so an agent picks it up the same way

- @Key idea@: "coding while you sleep" only closes the loop if a failure
  the agent caused while asleep finds its way back to an agent, not just
  to an on-call human

// TODO(gp): Look into Sentry/Rollbar/Datadog error-to-issue integrations and
//   PagerDuty-to-GitHub bridges as the tools that would close this loop

* Canary Analysis and Automated Rollback

- @Problem@: `docker_rollback_dev_image` exists, but rollback is a command
  a human decides to run after noticing something is wrong; nothing
  decides automatically that something is wrong

- @Definition@: a **canary release** ships a change to a small slice of
  traffic first and compares its error rate and latency against the
  unchanged majority before widening the rollout

- @Gap@: nothing in this stack automates that comparison or the rollback it
  should trigger; the release flow (local $\to$ dev $\to$ prod) is staged
  by environment, not by traffic percentage

- @What it would take@
  - A metric comparison job that runs after each deploy and pages before a
    human would have noticed manually
  - A rollback triggered by a metric threshold, with the same
    `docker_rollback_dev_image` path a human uses today, so the automated
    and manual paths stay identical

- @Key idea@: the tooling already made rollback fast for a human to
  execute; it is not yet fast enough to execute itself

// TODO(gp): Look into Flagger and Argo Rollouts for canary automation on
//   Kubernetes, Istio traffic splitting, and Prometheus + Alertmanager for
//   SLO-triggered rollback

# Summary

* Why This Tooling Matters for AI-Assisted Development

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

- @Key idea@: nothing in this stack was built for agents, and that is the point
  - Discoverability, automation, standardization, and immediate feedback happen
    to be what an agent needs

- @Characteristics@: the properties that make a codebase agent-ready
  - _Self-documenting workflows_: `invoke --list` replaces tribal knowledge
  - _Manifests and contracts_: `CLAUDE.md` and `repo_config.yaml` answer
    questions instead of hiding answers in prose
  - _Context-bounded editing_: the runnable directory limits what must be read
  - _Guardrails and provenance_: hooks, linters, and scanners make failures
    detectable, and evidence makes changes reviewable
  - _Deterministic verification_: golden files and categorized tests turn
    correctness into a diff

- @Fact@: the questions removed are the ones agents answer badly
  - _"How should I format this?"_, _"Where do tests live?"_, _"How do I run
    them?"_

- @Remark@: the loop is self-reinforcing, since better infrastructure enables
  better assistance, which pays for more infrastructure

// TODO(gp): Look into agent-readiness scoring frameworks and benchmarks
//   (SWE-bench, METR evals) to quantify how agent-friendly a stack actually is

* Tooling Stack: Summary and Open Questions

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/resources.md

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Lifecycle stage", "Artifact the agent uses"),
  rows: (
    ("Plan the work", "specs.write, ADRs (gap)"),
    ("Understand the repo", "CLAUDE.md, rules files, repo_config.yaml"),
    ("Do a task", "Skill files, invoke tasks"),
    ("Change code", "Runnable directory boundary, linter"),
    ("Prove the change", "Fast/slow tests, golden files, coverage, mutation (gap)"),
    ("Deliver", "PR split plan, ai_review.py, CI gates"),
    ("Release", "Docker stages, changelog.txt, registries"),
    ("Learn from production", "Incident-to-issue, canary/rollback (gap)"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.7fr),
)
```

- @Question@: how much autonomy should each gate allow before a human signs off?

- @Question@: when the linter and the model disagree about style, which one is
  the specification?

- @Question@: rules files consume context, so how large can they grow before
  they cost more than they save?

- @Question@: who owns a regression introduced by an agent-authored pull request
  that a human approved?

- @Key idea@: the tooling does not make the agent correct, it makes the agent
  _checkable_, which is the property a team can actually build on

